(FIL-89: a program that exits with a buffer un-freed and a file handle un-closed exits cleanly, and the bytes it wrote are on disk.
 Also covers: FIL-84, FIL-88, FIL-98, FIL-99.
 Only the observable half of the automatic-cleanup claim. Whether the FD
 was actually released at exit is not observable from inside Vox - that is
 FIL-95, and it is marked not assertable for exactly this reason.
 Write is unbuffered here: a reader opened in the same program before any
 Close already sees the bytes, so surviving exit is what this proves.
 Run from the vox-fuzz worktree root; the probe creates everything it
 needs under vf_scratch/files/ and touches nothing else.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-89.vox -o p && ./p
 actual output:
   exiting without a Close - the runtime must close the handle
)
Create a directory called "vf_scratch".
Create a directory called "vf_scratch/files".
a buffer called data is 32 bytes in size.
append "auto-freed on exit" to data.
open a file for writing called log at "vf_scratch/files/never-closed.txt".
Write "written but never closed" to log.
print "exiting without a Close - the runtime must close the handle".
